1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,475
Chapter 36: The Puerperium.

3
00:00:05,475 --> 00:00:23,037
The Fourth Trimester Reproductive Tract Involution Placental Site Involution Urinary Tract Peritoneum And Abdominal Wall Blood And Blood Volume Lactation And Breastfeeding Hospital Care Home Care References The word puerperium is derived from Latin—puer, child + parus, bringing forth.

4
00:00:23,037 --> 00:00:32,287
It defines the time following delivery during which pregnancy-induced maternal anatomical and physiological changes return to the nonpregnant state.

5
00:00:32,287 --> 00:00:37,650
Its duration is inexact but is considered to last between 4 and 6 weeks.

6
00:00:37,650 --> 00:00:46,637
Although much less complex compared with pregnancy, the puerperium has appreciable changes, and maternal morbidity is surprisingly common.

7
00:00:46,637 --> 00:00:54,800
For example, in a survey of 1246 British mothers, 3 percent required hospital readmission within 8 weeks .

8
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:00,962
Moreover, almost three fourths of women continue to have health problems for up to 18 months .

9
00:01:00,962 --> 00:01:07,250
Of self-reported concerns, pain, breastfeeding, and psychosocial topics are prominent.

10
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:18,125
Table 36-1 lists data on these from the Pregnancy Risk Assessment Surveillance System—PRAMS—of the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC).

11
00:01:18,125 --> 00:01:20,687
Table 36-1.

12
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:25,175
Concerns Raised by Women in the Puerperium The Fourth Trimester.

13
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:38,150
Because the weeks following childbirth are a critical period for the woman and her infant, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a) promulgated the concept of a “fourth trimester.

14
00:01:38,150 --> 00:01:47,150
This concept reinforces the importance of the 12 weeks following birth, and components of this model are outlined in Table 36-2.

15
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:54,862
Thus, the comprehensive postpartum visit includes a full assessment of physical, social, and psychological well-being.

16
00:01:54,862 --> 00:01:57,400
Table 36-2.

17
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:06,812
Components of Postpartum Care for the Fourth Trimester An initial visit is recommended at 3 weeks postdelivery and a final summary visit at 12 weeks.

18
00:02:06,812 --> 00:02:10,550
Between this time, visits can be added as needed.

19
00:02:10,550 --> 00:02:21,637
For example, women with chronic hypertension, overt diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and depression may require additional multidisciplinary care during this period.

20
00:02:21,637 --> 00:02:27,162
For all puerpera, a discussion of positive lifestyle changes can be initiated.

21
00:02:27,162 --> 00:02:31,900
This time also affords the opportunity to update immunizations .

22
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:43,137
At the end of the 12-week fourth trimester, follow-up then transitions into well-woman care, ongoing primary or specialty care, and when necessary, preconceptional counseling .

23
00:02:43,137 --> 00:02:45,862
Reproductive Tract Involution.

24
00:02:45,862 --> 00:02:48,112
# Birth Canal.

25
00:02:48,112 --> 00:02:53,900
Return of the tissues in the birth canal to the nonpregnant state begins soon after delivery.

26
00:02:53,900 --> 00:03:00,400
The vagina and its outlet gradually diminish in size but rarely regain their nulliparous dimensions.

27
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:05,487
Rugae begin to reappear by the third week but are less prominent than before.

28
00:03:05,487 --> 00:03:12,262
The hymen is represented by several small tags of tissue, which scar to form the myrtiform caruncles.

29
00:03:12,262 --> 00:03:20,287
The vaginal epithelium reflects the hypoestrogenic state, and it does not begin to proliferate until 4 to 6 weeks.

30
00:03:20,287 --> 00:03:25,625
This timing is usually coincidental with resumed ovarian estrogen production.

31
00:03:25,625 --> 00:03:32,062
Lacerations or stretching of the perineum during delivery can lead to vaginal outlet relaxation.

32
00:03:32,062 --> 00:03:39,175
Some damage to the pelvic floor may be inevitable, and parturition predisposes to pelvic organ prolapse .

33
00:03:39,175 --> 00:03:41,325
# Uterus.

34
00:03:41,325 --> 00:03:50,537
The massively augmented uterine blood flow necessary to maintain pregnancy derives from significant hypertrophy and remodeling of pelvic vessels.

35
00:03:50,537 --> 00:03:57,037
After delivery, their caliber gradually diminishes to approximately that of the prepregnant state.

36
00:03:57,037 --> 00:04:03,087
Within the puerperal uterus, larger blood vessels become obliterated by hyaline changes.

37
00:04:03,087 --> 00:04:07,225
They are gradually resorbed and replaced by smaller ones.

38
00:04:07,225 --> 00:04:12,300
Minor vestiges of the larger vessels, however, may persist for years.

39
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:20,950
During labor and vaginal delivery, the margin of the dilated cervix, which corresponds to the external os, may be lacerated.

40
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:28,687
The cervical opening contracts slowly, and for a few days immediately after labor, it readily admits two fingers.

41
00:04:28,687 --> 00:04:35,850
By the end of the first week, this opening narrows, the cervix thickens, and the endocervical canal reforms.

42
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:40,625
The external os does not completely resume its pregravid appearance.

43
00:04:40,625 --> 00:04:42,387
It remains

44
00:04:42,556 --> 00:04:49,156
somewhat wider, and typically, ectocervical depressions at the site of lacerations become permanent.

45
00:04:49,106 --> 00:04:53,081
These changes are characteristic of a parous cervix .

46
00:04:53,081 --> 00:04:57,543
Cervical epithelium also undergoes considerable remodeling.

47
00:04:57,543 --> 00:05:09,043
This actually may be salutary because almost half of women have regression of high-grade dysplasia following delivery (Ahdoot, 1998; Kaneshiro, 2005).

48
00:05:09,043 --> 00:05:11,668
Figure 36-1.

49
00:05:11,668 --> 00:05:16,793
Common appearance of nulliparous (A) and parous (B) cervices.

50
00:05:16,793 --> 00:05:22,793
After delivery, the fundus of the contracted uterus lies slightly below the umbilicus.

51
00:05:22,793 --> 00:05:28,931
It consists mostly of myometrium covered by serosa and internally lined by decidua.

52
00:05:28,931 --> 00:05:36,593
The markedly attenuated lower uterine segment contracts and retracts, but not as forcefully as the uterine corpus.

53
00:05:36,593 --> 00:05:50,043
During the next few weeks, the lower segment is converted from a clearly distinct substructure large enough to accommodate the fetal head to a barely discernible uterine isthmus located between the corpus and internal cervical os.

54
00:05:50,043 --> 00:05:58,693
Immediately postpartum, the anterior and posterior walls, which lie in close apposition, are each 4 to 5 cm thick .

55
00:05:58,693 --> 00:06:10,856
At this time, the uterus weighs approximately 1000 g. Myometrial involution is a truly remarkable feat of destruction or deconstruction that begins as soon as 2 days after delivery .

56
00:06:10,856 --> 00:06:17,418
The total number of myocytes does not decline appreciably—rather, their size decreases markedly.

57
00:06:17,418 --> 00:06:30,206
Weights from removed uteri approximate 500 g by 1 week postpartum, 300 g by 2 weeks, and at 4 weeks, involution is complete and the uterus weighs approximately 100 g .

58
00:06:30,206 --> 00:06:37,181
After each successive delivery, the uterus is usually slightly larger than before the most recent pregnancy.

59
00:06:37,181 --> 00:06:43,843
Sonographic Findings Uterine involution and declining uterine volume is best measured by sonography .

60
00:06:43,843 --> 00:06:52,693
With this, estimates again show uterine size declines of more than half, from 450 to 200 g, in the first 2 weeks.

61
00:06:52,693 --> 00:06:59,618
Sonographically, the uterus and endometrium return to the pregravid size by 8 weeks postpartum.

62
00:06:59,618 --> 00:07:04,968
Complete involution in the multiparous uterus is longer than for the nulliparous organ .

63
00:07:04,968 --> 00:07:13,781
In the event of a cesarean delivery, the residual myometrial thickness over the uterine incision is thicker if a two-layer closure was used .

64
00:07:13,781 --> 00:07:16,293
Figure 36-2.

65
00:07:16,293 --> 00:07:22,031
Pattern of uterine involution through 7 weeks postpartum as estimated by sonography.

66
00:07:22,031 --> 00:07:36,106
In a study of 42 normal puerperas, fluid in the endometrial cavity was noted in 78 percent of women at 2 weeks, 52 percent at 3 weeks, 30 percent at 4 weeks, and 10 percent at 5 weeks .

67
00:07:36,106 --> 00:07:52,181
Belachew and coworkers (2012) used three-dimensional sonography and visualized intracavitary tissue matter in a third on day 1, 95 percent on day 7, 87 percent on day 14, and 28 percent on day 28.

68
00:07:52,181 --> 00:07:55,943
By day 56, the small cavity was empty.

69
00:07:55,943 --> 00:08:08,356
Doppler ultrasound of the uterine artery shows continuously increasing vascular resistance throughout the first 7 weeks postpartum (Sohn, 1988; Wataganara, 2015).

70
00:08:08,356 --> 00:08:15,118
Uterine artery flow impedance does not differ between women undergoing vaginal versus cesarean delivery .

71
00:08:15,118 --> 00:08:24,893
Decidual and Endometrial Regeneration Because separation of the placenta and membranes involves the spongy layer, the decidua basalis is not sloughed.

72
00:08:24,893 --> 00:08:34,743
The in situ decidua varies markedly in thickness, it has an irregular jagged border, and it is infiltrated with blood, especially at the placental site.

73
00:08:34,743 --> 00:08:41,581
Within 2 or 3 days after delivery, the remaining decidua becomes differentiated into two layers.

74
00:08:41,581 --> 00:08:46,106
The superficial layer becomes necrotic and is sloughed in the lochia.

75
00:08:46,106 --> 00:08:52,356
The basal layer adjacent to the myometrium remains intact and is the source of new endometrium.

76
00:08:52,356 --> 00:08:58,493
Decidual vessels are near normal by delivery and endovascular trophoblasts are diminished.

77
00:08:58,493 --> 00:09:03,068
These vessels and the spiral arteries also undergo involution .

78
00:09:03,068 --> 00:09:08,031
Endometrial regeneration is rapid, except at the placental site.

79
00:09:08,031 --> 00:09:12,931
Within a week or so, the free surface becomes covered by epithelium.

80
00:09:12,931 --> 00:09:22,006
Sharman (1953) identified a fully restored endometrium in all biopsy specimens obtained from the 16th day onward.

81
00:09:22,006 --> 00:09:26,856
Histological endometritis is part of the normal reparative process.

82
00:09:26,856 --> 00:09:33,681
Also, microscopic inflammatory changes characteristic of noninfectious acute salpingitis

83
00:09:33,844 --> 00:09:38,131
are seen in almost half of women between 5 and 15 days .

84
00:09:38,081 --> 00:09:40,906
Clinical Aspects Afterpains.

85
00:09:40,906 --> 00:09:45,269
Several clinical findings coincide with uterine involution.

86
00:09:45,269 --> 00:09:51,069
In primiparas, the uterus tends to remain tonically contracted following delivery.

87
00:09:51,069 --> 00:10:00,981
In multiparas, however, it often contracts vigorously at intervals and gives rise to afterpains, which are similar to but milder than labor contractions.

88
00:10:00,981 --> 00:10:08,331
These are more pronounced as parity increases and worsen when the newborn suckles, likely because of oxytocin release .

89
00:10:08,331 --> 00:10:13,719
Usually, afterpains decrease in intensity and become mild by the third day.

90
00:10:13,719 --> 00:10:20,756
We have encountered unusually severe and persistent afterpains in women with postpartum uterine infections .

91
00:10:20,756 --> 00:10:22,356
Lochia.

92
00:10:22,356 --> 00:10:29,356
Early in the puerperium, sloughing of decidual tissue results in a vaginal discharge of variable quantity.

93
00:10:29,356 --> 00:10:37,231
The discharge is termed lochia and contains erythrocytes, shredded decidua, epithelial cells, and bacteria.

94
00:10:37,231 --> 00:10:43,494
For the first few days after delivery, there is blood sufficient to color it red— lochia rubra.

95
00:10:43,494 --> 00:10:49,794
After 3 or 4 days, lochia becomes progressively pale in color —lochia serosa.

96
00:10:49,794 --> 00:11:00,006
After approximately the 10th day, because of an admixture of leukocytes and reduced fluid content, lochia assumes a white or yellowwhite color—lochia alba.

97
00:11:00,006 --> 00:11:05,769
The average duration of lochial discharge ranges from 24 to 36 days .

98
00:11:05,769 --> 00:11:16,906
Because of this expected leukocyte component, saline preparations of lochia for microscopic evaluation in cases of suspected puerperal metritis are typically uninformative.

99
00:11:16,906 --> 00:11:19,356
Placental Site Involution.

100
00:11:19,356 --> 00:11:23,781
Complete extrusion of the placental site takes up to 6 weeks .

101
00:11:23,781 --> 00:11:29,081
Immediately after delivery, the placental site is approximately palmsized.

102
00:11:29,081 --> 00:11:36,281
Within hours of delivery, it normally contains many thrombosed vessels that ultimately undergo organization.

103
00:11:36,281 --> 00:11:41,544
By the end of the second week, it measures 3 to 4 cm in diameter.

104
00:11:41,544 --> 00:11:51,756
Placental site involution is an exfoliation process, which is prompted in great part by undermining of the implantation site by new endometrial proliferation .

105
00:11:51,756 --> 00:11:55,894
Thus, involution is not simply absorption in situ.

106
00:11:55,894 --> 00:12:10,956
Exfoliation consists of both extension and “downgrowth” of endometrium from the margins of the placental site, as well as “upward” development of endometrial tissue from the glands and stroma left deep in the decidua basalis after placental separation.

107
00:12:10,956 --> 00:12:22,631
Anderson and Davis (1968) concluded that placental site exfoliation results from sloughing of infarcted and necrotic superficial tissues followed by a remodeling process.

108
00:12:22,631 --> 00:12:25,094
# Subinvolution.

109
00:12:25,094 --> 00:12:34,456
In some cases, uterine involution is hindered because of incompletely remodeled spiral arteries, retained placental fragments, or infection.

110
00:12:34,456 --> 00:12:42,056
Such subinvolution is accompanied by varied intervals of prolonged lochia and by irregular or excessive uterine bleeding.

111
00:12:42,056 --> 00:12:47,969
During bimanual examination, the uterus is larger and softer than would be expected.

112
00:12:47,969 --> 00:12:54,931
Inadequate conversion of spiral arteries into remodeled uteroplacental arteries can cause subinvolution .

113
00:12:54,931 --> 00:13:00,669
These noninvoluted vessels are filled with thromboses and lack an endothelial lining.

114
00:13:00,669 --> 00:13:10,306
Perivascular trophoblasts are also identified in the vessel walls, which suggests an aberrant interaction between uterine and trophoblast cells.

115
00:13:10,306 --> 00:13:16,569
With bleeding, pelvic sonography may help differentiate subinvolution from retained placenta.

116
00:13:16,569 --> 00:13:22,919
Characteristic findings of retained products include a thickened endometrium or endometrial mass .

117
00:13:22,919 --> 00:13:28,331
Vascularity within this area increases the likelihood of retained products .

118
00:13:28,331 --> 00:13:36,394
Comparatively, subinvolution is characterized by an enlarged uterus with tubular hypoechoic areas in the myometrium.

119
00:13:36,394 --> 00:13:42,269
These tubular structures reflect neovascularization and dilated uterine vessels.

120
00:13:42,269 --> 00:13:44,794
Figure 36-3.

121
00:13:44,794 --> 00:13:48,956
Sonographic appearance of puerperal uterine complications.

122
00:13:48,956 --> 00:13:51,956
A. Retained products of conception.

123
00:13:51,956 --> 00:13:56,031
Tissue vascularity is highlighted here by color Doppler.

124
00:13:56,031 --> 00:14:02,856
B. Retained clot marked by calipers shows mixed echogenicity in a woman with subinvolutional hemorrhage.

125
00:14:02,856 --> 00:14:08,481
Anechoic cystic areas within the fundal myometrium reflect dilated vessels.

126
00:14:08,481 --> 00:14:15,969
For treatment, uterotonics are recommended by many but their efficacy is questionable (Hoyveda, 2001;

127
00:14:16,132 --> 00:14:21,582
American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2017d).

128
00:14:21,532 --> 00:14:33,619
At Parkland hospital, conservative management is undertaken with methylergonovine (Methergine) 0.2 mg orally every 3 to 4 hours for 24 to 48 hours.

129
00:14:33,619 --> 00:14:44,882
For initially brisk bleeding, an intramuscular dose of methylergonovine may be coupled with an infusion of synthetic oxytocin (Pitocin) (20 units in 1 L crystalloid).

130
00:14:44,882 --> 00:14:52,082
For cases with suspected comorbid infection, antimicrobial therapy usually leads to a good response.

131
00:14:52,082 --> 00:15:00,607
Wager and associates (1980) reported that a third of late cases of postpartum metritis are caused by Chlamydia trachomatis.

132
00:15:00,607 --> 00:15:08,519
Empirical therapy with azithromycin or doxycycline usually prompts resolution regardless of bacterial etiology.

133
00:15:08,519 --> 00:15:26,957
At our institution, common oral options taken twice daily for 7 to 10 days include azithromycin, 500 mg; amoxicillin-clavulanate (Augmentin), 875 mg; or doxycycline, 100 mg. # Late Postpartum Hemorrhage.

134
00:15:26,957 --> 00:15:38,144
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017d) defines secondary postpartum hemorrhage as bleeding 24 hours to 12 weeks after delivery.

135
00:15:38,144 --> 00:15:44,469
Clinically worrisome uterine hemorrhage develops within 1 to 2 weeks in perhaps 1 percent of women.

136
00:15:44,469 --> 00:15:49,757
Such bleeding most often stems from abnormal involution of the placental site.

137
00:15:49,757 --> 00:15:56,244
It occasionally is caused by retention of a placental fragment or by a uterine artery pseudoaneurysm.

138
00:15:56,244 --> 00:16:05,582
Rarely, retained products may undergo necrosis with fibrin deposition and neovascularization, thus forming a placental polypoid mass.

139
00:16:05,582 --> 00:16:11,482
Severe hemorrhage occurs in only 6 percent of these cases due to rupture of blood vessels .

140
00:16:11,482 --> 00:16:19,294
Sonographically, a discrete hypervascular endometrial mass that may extend into the myometrium is visualized .

141
00:16:19,294 --> 00:16:30,532
As discussed in Chapter 59 (p. 1062), delayed postpartum hemorrhage may also be caused by von Willebrand disease or other inherited coagulopathies .

142
00:16:30,532 --> 00:16:33,219
Figure 36-4.

143
00:16:33,219 --> 00:16:37,932
Sonogram of a polypoid placental mass with color Doppler applied.

144
00:16:37,932 --> 00:16:41,519
The mass is demarcated by the dashed white lines.

145
00:16:41,519 --> 00:16:46,432
Hypervascularity is seen extending from the mass into the myometrium.

146
00:16:46,432 --> 00:16:52,744
In our experiences, few women with delayed hemorrhage are found to have retained placental fragments.

147
00:16:52,744 --> 00:16:57,194
Thus, we and others do not routinely perform curettage .

148
00:16:57,194 --> 00:17:03,482
Another concern is that curettage may worsen bleeding by avulsing part of the implantation site.

149
00:17:03,482 --> 00:17:13,844
Thus, in a stable patient, if sonographic examination shows an empty cavity, then oxytocin, methylergonovine, or a prostaglandin analogue is given.

150
00:17:13,844 --> 00:17:19,044
Suitable dosing is found in Table 13-3 (p. 240).

151
00:17:19,044 --> 00:17:23,344
Antimicrobials are added if uterine infection is suspected.

152
00:17:23,344 --> 00:17:30,169
If large clots are seen in the uterine cavity with sonography, then gentle suction curettage is considered.

153
00:17:30,169 --> 00:17:37,594
Otherwise, curettage is carried out only if appreciable bleeding persists or recurs after medical management.

154
00:17:37,594 --> 00:17:39,657
Urinary Tract.

155
00:17:39,657 --> 00:17:48,319
Normal pregnancy-induced glomerular hyperfiltration persists during the puerperium but returns to prepregnancy baseline by 2 weeks .

156
00:17:48,319 --> 00:17:53,857
Parturition induces a transient rise in excretion of glomerular podocytes .

157
00:17:53,857 --> 00:18:00,507
Dilated ureters and renal pelves return to their prepregnant state by 2 to 8 weeks postpartum.

158
00:18:00,507 --> 00:18:12,419
Because of this dilated collecting system, coupled with residual urine and bacteriuria in a traumatized bladder, symptomatic urinary tract infection remains a concern in the puerperium.

159
00:18:12,419 --> 00:18:21,869
Funnell and colleagues (1954) used cystoscopy immediately postpartum and described varying degrees of submucosal hemorrhage and edema.

160
00:18:21,869 --> 00:18:29,282
Bladder trauma is associated most closely with labor length and thus to some degree accompanies normal vaginal delivery.

161
00:18:29,282 --> 00:18:36,057
Postpartum, the bladder has a greater capacity and a relative insensitivity to intravesical pressure.

162
00:18:36,057 --> 00:18:46,632
Consequently, overdistention, incomplete emptying, and excessive residual urine are frequent (Buchanan, 2014; Mulder, 2014).

163
00:18:46,632 --> 00:18:52,332
Acute urinary retention is more common with epidural or narcotic analgesia .

164
00:18:52,332 --> 00:18:57,844
The management of urinary retention is discussed later (p. 643).

165
00:18:57,844 --> 00:19:03,532
Stress urinary incontinence during the puerperium may occur in 5 percent of women .

166
00:19:03,532 --> 00:19:10,057
Much attention has been given to the potential for subsequent development of urinary incontinence and other

167
00:19:10,228 --> 00:19:13,978
pelvic floor disorders in the years following delivery .

168
00:19:13,928 --> 00:19:19,128
A more detailed discussion is found in Chapter 30 (p. 548).

169
00:19:19,128 --> 00:19:21,915
Peritoneum And Abdominal Wall.

170
00:19:21,915 --> 00:19:28,690
The broad and round ligaments require considerable time to recover from stretching and loosening during pregnancy.

171
00:19:28,690 --> 00:19:37,565
The abdominal wall remains soft and flaccid as a result of ruptured elastic fibers in the skin and prolonged distention by the gravid uterus.

172
00:19:37,565 --> 00:19:43,978
If the abdomen is unusually lax or pendulous, an ordinary girdle is often satisfactory.

173
00:19:43,978 --> 00:19:47,578
An abdominal binder is another temporary measure.

174
00:19:47,578 --> 00:19:53,865
Several weeks are required for these structures to return to normal, and exercise aids recovery.

175
00:19:53,865 --> 00:19:57,978
These may be started anytime following vaginal delivery.

176
00:19:57,978 --> 00:20:07,290
After cesarean delivery, a 6-week interval to allow anterior abdominal wall fascia to heal and abdominal soreness to diminish is reasonable.

177
00:20:07,290 --> 00:20:12,053
Silvery abdominal striae commonly develop as striae gravidarum .

178
00:20:12,053 --> 00:20:17,640
Except for these, the abdominal wall usually resumes its prepregnancy appearance.

179
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:23,290
When muscles remain atonic, however, the abdominal wall also remains lax.

180
00:20:23,290 --> 00:20:29,115
Marked separation of the rectus abdominis muscles—diastasis recti— may result.

181
00:20:29,115 --> 00:20:38,065
This separation develops from a gradual thinning and widening of the linea alba and is coupled with a general laxity of the ventral abdominal wall muscles.

182
00:20:38,065 --> 00:20:45,515
Importantly, musculofascial continuity and lack of a true hernia sac differentiates this from a ventral hernia .

183
00:20:45,515 --> 00:20:47,690
Blood And Blood Volume.

184
00:20:47,690 --> 00:20:51,703
# Hematological and Coagulation Changes.

185
00:20:51,703 --> 00:20:57,078
Marked leukocytosis and thrombocytosis may occur during and after labor.

186
00:20:57,078 --> 00:21:10,190
The white blood cell count seldom exceeds 25,000/μL, and the rise is predominantly due to granulocytes (Arbib, 2016; Sanci, 2017).

187
00:21:10,190 --> 00:21:15,153
A relative lymphopenia and an absolute eosinopenia is typical.

188
00:21:15,153 --> 00:21:22,703
Normally, during the first few postpartum days, hemoglobin concentration and hematocrit decrease moderately.

189
00:21:22,703 --> 00:21:28,478
If they fall much below prelabor levels, a considerable amount of blood has been lost.

190
00:21:28,478 --> 00:21:34,015
By the end of pregnancy, laboratory values that assess coagulation are altered .

191
00:21:34,015 --> 00:21:41,728
These changes are discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 61) and listed in the Appendix (p. 1228).

192
00:21:41,728 --> 00:21:45,090
Many extend variably in the puerperium.

193
00:21:45,090 --> 00:21:51,453
For example, a markedly higher plasma fibrinogen level is maintained at least through the first week.

194
00:21:51,453 --> 00:21:54,778
This contributes to hypercoagulability .

195
00:21:54,778 --> 00:22:04,128
As one result, the pregnancy-associated risks for deep-vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism persist in the 12 weeks following childbirth .

196
00:22:04,128 --> 00:22:07,490
# Pregnancy-Induced Hypervolemia.

197
00:22:07,490 --> 00:22:17,603
When the amount of blood attained by normal pregnancy hypervolemia is lost as postpartum hemorrhage, the woman almost immediately regains her nonpregnant blood volume .

198
00:22:17,603 --> 00:22:25,740
If less has been lost at delivery, blood volume generally nearly returns to its nonpregnant level by 1 week after delivery.

199
00:22:25,740 --> 00:22:34,803
Cardiac output usually remains elevated for 24 to 48 hours postpartum and declines to nonpregnant values by 10 days .

200
00:22:34,803 --> 00:22:41,303
Heart rate changes follow this pattern, and blood pressure similarly returns to nonpregnant values .

201
00:22:41,303 --> 00:22:53,153
Correspondingly, systemic vascular resistance remains in the lower range characteristic of pregnancy for 2 days postpartum and then begins to steadily rise to normal nonpregnant values .

202
00:22:53,153 --> 00:23:01,603
Despite this, Morris and coworkers (2015) found that reduced arterial stiffness persists following pregnancy.

203
00:23:01,603 --> 00:23:08,015
They suggest a significant favorable effect of pregnancy on maternal cardiovascular remodeling.

204
00:23:08,015 --> 00:23:14,353
This may represent a mechanism by which preeclampsia risk is reduced in subsequent pregnancies.

205
00:23:14,353 --> 00:23:17,003
Figure 36-5.

206
00:23:17,003 --> 00:23:23,028
During the early puerperium, blood pressure normally rises toward nonpregnant values.

207
00:23:23,028 --> 00:23:26,015
MAP = mean arterial pressure.

208
00:23:26,015 --> 00:23:29,015
# Postpartum Diuresis.

209
00:23:29,015 --> 00:23:39,465
Normal pregnancy is associated with appreciable extracellular sodium and water retention, and postpartum diuresis is a physiological reversal of this process.

210
00:23:39,465 --> 00:23:48,453
Chesley and associates (1959) demonstrated an approximate 2 liter decline in sodium space during the first week postpartum.

211
00:23:48,453 --> 00:23:52,978
This corresponds with loss of residual pregnancy hypervolemia.

212
00:23:52,978 --> 00:24:01,153
In preeclampsia, pathological retention of fluid antepartum and then its normal diuresis postpartum may be prodigious .

213
00:24:01,324 --> 00:24:12,749
Postpartum diuresis results in a relatively rapid weight loss of 2 to 3 kg, which is additive to the 5 to 6 kg incurred by delivery and normal blood loss.

214
00:24:12,699 --> 00:24:18,311
Weight loss from pregnancy itself likely peaks by the end of the second week postpartum.

215
00:24:18,311 --> 00:24:24,899
Any residual weight compared with prepregnancy values probably represents fat stores that will persist.

216
00:24:24,899 --> 00:24:27,361
Lactation And Breastfeeding.

217
00:24:27,361 --> 00:24:31,186
# Breast Anatomy and Secretory Products.

218
00:24:31,186 --> 00:24:36,524
Each mature mammary gland or breast is composed of 15 to 25 lobes.

219
00:24:36,524 --> 00:24:42,036
They are arranged radially and are separated from one another by varying amounts of fat.

220
00:24:42,036 --> 00:24:48,061
Each lobe consists of several lobules, which in turn are composed of numerous alveoli.

221
00:24:48,061 --> 00:24:54,724
Each alveolus is provided with a small duct that joins others to form a single larger duct for each lobe .

222
00:24:54,724 --> 00:25:01,999
These lactiferous ducts open separately on the nipple, where they may be distinguished as small but distinct orifices.

223
00:25:01,999 --> 00:25:07,461
The alveolar secretory epithelium synthesizes the various milk constituents.

224
00:25:07,461 --> 00:25:10,174
Figure 36-6.

225
00:25:10,174 --> 00:25:14,536
Schematic of the alveolar and ductal system during lactation.

226
00:25:14,536 --> 00:25:20,911
Note the myoepithelial fibers (M) that surround the outside of the uppermost alveolus.

227
00:25:20,911 --> 00:25:32,599
The secretions from the glandular elements are extruded into the lumen of the alveoli (A) and ejected by the myoepithelial cells into the ductal system (D), which empties through the nipple.

228
00:25:32,599 --> 00:25:39,974
Arterial blood supply to the alveolus is identified by the upper right arrow and venous drainage by the arrow beneath.

229
00:25:39,974 --> 00:25:45,611
After delivery, the breasts begin to secrete colostrum, which is a deepyellow liquid.

230
00:25:45,611 --> 00:25:50,311
It usually can be expressed from the nipples by the second postpartum day.

231
00:25:50,311 --> 00:25:58,161
Compared with mature milk, colostrum is rich in immunological components and contains more minerals and amino acids .

232
00:25:58,161 --> 00:26:03,536
It also has more protein, much of which is globulin, but less sugar and fat.

233
00:26:03,536 --> 00:26:10,949
Secretion persists for 5 to 14 days, with gradual conversion to mature milk by 4 to 6 weeks.

234
00:26:10,949 --> 00:26:18,624
The colostrum content of immunoglobulin A (IgA) offers the newborn protection against enteric pathogens.

235
00:26:18,624 --> 00:26:29,124
Other host resistance factors found in colostrum and milk include complement, macrophages, lymphocytes, lactoferrin, lactoperoxidase, and lysozymes.

236
00:26:29,124 --> 00:26:39,011
A lactating mother produces upwards of 600 mL of milk daily, and maternal gestational weight gain has little impact on its quantity or quality.

237
00:26:39,011 --> 00:26:51,711
Mature milk is a complex and dynamic biological fluid that includes fat, proteins, carbohydrates, bioactive factors, minerals, vitamins, hormones, and many cellular products .

238
00:26:51,711 --> 00:27:01,586
The contents and concentrations of human milk change even during a single feed and are influenced by maternal diet and by newborn age, health, and needs.

239
00:27:01,586 --> 00:27:07,474
Milk is isotonic with plasma, and lactose accounts for half of the osmotic pressure.

240
00:27:07,474 --> 00:27:16,699
Essential amino acids are derived from blood, and nonessential amino acids are derived in part from blood or synthesized in the mammary gland.

241
00:27:16,699 --> 00:27:23,311
Most milk proteins are unique and include alpha-lactalbumin, beta-lactoglobulin, and casein.

242
00:27:23,311 --> 00:27:30,136
Fatty acids are synthesized in the alveoli from glucose and are secreted by an apocrine-like process.

243
00:27:30,136 --> 00:27:34,311
Variable amounts of most vitamins are found in human milk.

244
00:27:34,311 --> 00:27:40,699
Vitamin K is virtually absent, and thus, an intramuscular dose is given to the newborn .

245
00:27:40,699 --> 00:27:53,086
Vitamin D content is low, and newborn supplementation is recommended by the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017).

246
00:27:53,086 --> 00:27:55,599
Table 36-3.

247
00:27:55,599 --> 00:28:03,749
Average Composition of Human Breast Milk Whey is milk serum and contains large amounts of interleukin-6 and other chemokines .

248
00:28:03,749 --> 00:28:10,211
Human milk has a whey-to-casein ratio of 60:40, which is considered ideal for absorption.

249
00:28:10,211 --> 00:28:14,424
Prolactin appears to be actively secreted into breast milk.

250
00:28:14,424 --> 00:28:26,599
Epidermal growth factor has been identified, and because it is not destroyed by gastric proteolytic enzymes, it may be absorbed to promote growth and maturation of newborn intestinal mucosa.

251
00:28:26,599 --> 00:28:32,211
Other critical components in human milk include melatonin and oligosaccharides.

252
00:28:32,211 --> 00:28:35,361
# Lactation Endocrinology.

253
00:28:35,361 --> 00:28:40,674
The precise humoral and neural mechanisms involved in lactation are complex.

254
00:28:40,674 --> 00:28:45,924
Progesterone, estrogen, and placental lactogen, as well as prolactin,

255
00:28:46,084 --> 00:28:53,709
cortisol, and insulin, appear to act in concert to stimulate the growth and development of the milk-secreting apparatus .

256
00:28:53,659 --> 00:29:00,571
With delivery, the maternal serum levels of progesterone and estrogen decline abruptly and profoundly.

257
00:29:00,571 --> 00:29:10,221
This drop removes the inhibitory influence of progesterone on alpha-lactalbumin production and stimulates lactose synthase to raise milk lactose levels.

258
00:29:10,221 --> 00:29:17,646
Progesterone withdrawal also allows prolactin to act unopposed in its stimulation of alpha-lactalbumin production.

259
00:29:17,646 --> 00:29:30,184
Activation of calcium-sensing receptors in mammary epithelial cells downregulates parathyroid hormone–related protein (PTHrP) and increases calcium transport into milk .

260
00:29:30,184 --> 00:29:36,896
Serotonin also is produced in mammary epithelial cells and has a role in maintaining milk production .

261
00:29:36,896 --> 00:29:46,634
The intensity and duration of subsequent lactation are controlled, in large part, by the repetitive stimulus of suckling and emptying of milk from the breast.

262
00:29:46,634 --> 00:29:54,821
Prolactin is essential for lactation, and women with extensive pituitary necrosis—Sheehan syndrome—do not lactate .

263
00:29:54,821 --> 00:30:03,534
Although plasma prolactin levels fall after delivery to levels lower than during pregnancy, each act of suckling triggers a rise in levels.

264
00:30:03,534 --> 00:30:12,459
Presumably a stimulus from the breast curtails the release of dopamine—also known as prolactin-inhibiting factor—from the hypothalamus.

265
00:30:12,459 --> 00:30:17,384
In turn, this transiently induces increased prolactin secretion.

266
00:30:17,384 --> 00:30:22,246
The posterior pituitary secretes oxytocin in pulsatile fashion.

267
00:30:22,246 --> 00:30:33,246
This stimulates milk expression from a lactating breast by causing contraction of myoepithelial cells in the alveoli and small milk ducts (see Fig. 36-6).

268
00:30:33,246 --> 00:30:43,059
Milk ejection, or letting down, is a reflex initiated especially by suckling, which stimulates the posterior pituitary to liberate oxytocin.

269
00:30:43,059 --> 00:30:49,609
The reflex may even be provoked by an infant cry and can be inhibited by maternal fright or stress .

270
00:30:49,609 --> 00:30:53,559
# Immunological Consequences of Breastfeeding.

271
00:30:53,559 --> 00:31:01,409
Human milk contains several protective immunological substances, including secretory IgA and growth factors.

272
00:31:01,409 --> 00:31:09,034
The antibodies in human milk are specifically directed against maternal environmental antigens such as Escherichia coli .

273
00:31:09,034 --> 00:31:21,684
According to the CDC, breastfeeding decreases the incidence of ear, respiratory, and gastrointestinal infections; necrotizing enterocolitis; and sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS) .

274
00:31:21,684 --> 00:31:26,384
Breastfeeding is especially important for immunity in preterm infants .

275
00:31:26,384 --> 00:31:33,859
Much attention has been directed to the role of maternal breast milk lymphocytes in neonatal immunological processes.

276
00:31:33,859 --> 00:31:40,771
Milk contains both T and B lymphocytes, but the T lymphocytes appear to differ from those found in blood.

277
00:31:40,771 --> 00:31:48,496
Specifically, milk T lymphocytes are almost exclusively composed of cells that exhibit specific membrane antigens.

278
00:31:48,496 --> 00:31:55,796
These memory T cells appear to be an avenue for the neonate to benefit from the maternal immunological experience.

279
00:31:55,796 --> 00:31:58,071
# Lactation.

280
00:31:58,071 --> 00:32:02,471
The ideal time to begin breastfeeding is within an hour of birth.

281
00:32:02,471 --> 00:32:11,871
Human milk is ideal food for newborns in that it provides age-specific nutrients, immunological factors, and antibacterial substances.

282
00:32:11,871 --> 00:32:19,009
Milk also contains factors that act as biological signals for promoting cellular growth and differentiation.

283
00:32:19,009 --> 00:32:24,184
A list of the advantages of breastfeeding is shown in Table 36-4.

284
00:32:24,184 --> 00:32:28,846
Lactation has long-term benefits for both the mother and the infant.

285
00:32:28,846 --> 00:32:34,671
For example, women who breastfeed have a lower risk of breast and reproductive cancer.

286
00:32:34,671 --> 00:32:46,009
Children who were breastfed have higher adult intelligence scores independent of a wide range of possible confounding factors (Jong, 2012; Kramer, 2008).

287
00:32:46,009 --> 00:32:51,396
In the short term, lactation is associated with less postpartum weight retention .

288
00:32:51,396 --> 00:32:56,809
In addition, rates of SIDS are significantly lower among breast-fed infants.

289
00:32:56,809 --> 00:33:09,021
Bartek and colleagues (2013) estimate that a 90-percent breastfeeding rate for 12 months would save more than $3 billion annually in excess infant and maternal morbidity costs.

290
00:33:09,021 --> 00:33:24,796
For all these reasons, the American Academy of Pediatrics and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) support the World Health Organization (2011) recommendations of exclusive breastfeeding for up to

291
00:33:24,964 --> 00:33:26,826
6 months.

292
00:33:26,776 --> 00:33:29,351
Table 36-4.

293
00:33:29,351 --> 00:33:39,039
Advantages of Breastfeeding Currently, 55 percent of women breastfeed at 6 months compared with a Healthy People 2020 goal of 61 percent.

294
00:33:39,039 --> 00:33:47,151
The Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative is an international program to raise rates of exclusive breastfeeding and to extend its duration.

295
00:33:47,151 --> 00:33:53,864
It is based on the World Health Organization (2018) Ten Steps to Successful Breastfeeding .

296
00:33:53,864 --> 00:34:08,901
Worldwide, almost 20,000 hospitals are deemed “baby-friendly,” however, only 15 to 20 percent of hospitals in the United States are so designated (Baby-Friendly USA, 2018; Perrine, 2015).

297
00:34:08,901 --> 00:34:18,226
In a large population-based study done in the United States, fewer than two thirds of term neonates were exclusively breastfed at the time of discharge .

298
00:34:18,226 --> 00:34:24,751
By 3 months, according to the CDC, less than half of these infants are exclusively breastfed .

299
00:34:24,751 --> 00:34:27,389
Table 36-5.

300
00:34:27,389 --> 00:34:45,364
Ten Steps to Successful Breastfeeding Various individual resources are available online for lactating mothers from the American Academy of Pediatrics (www.aap.org) and La Leche League International (www.llli.org).

301
00:34:45,364 --> 00:34:47,564
# Breast Care.

302
00:34:47,564 --> 00:34:53,001
The nipples require little attention other than cleanliness and attention to skin fissures.

303
00:34:53,001 --> 00:34:59,626
Fissured nipples render lactation painful, and they may have a deleterious influence on milk production.

304
00:34:59,626 --> 00:35:04,576
These cracks also provide a portal of entry for pyogenic bacteria.

305
00:35:04,576 --> 00:35:13,751
Because dried milk is likely to accumulate and irritate the nipples, washing the areola with water and mild soap is helpful before and after nursing.

306
00:35:13,751 --> 00:35:21,451
When the nipples are irritated or fissured, some recommend topical lanolin and a nipple shield for 24 hours or longer .

307
00:35:21,451 --> 00:35:27,076
If fissuring is severe, the newborn should not be permitted to nurse on the affected side.

308
00:35:27,076 --> 00:35:32,376
Instead, the breast is emptied regularly with a pump until the lesions are healed.

309
00:35:32,376 --> 00:35:36,664
Poor latching of the neonate to the breast can create such fissures.

310
00:35:36,664 --> 00:35:44,439
For example, the newborn may take into its mouth only the nipple, which is then is forced against the hard palate during suckling.

311
00:35:44,439 --> 00:35:50,676
Ideally, the nipple and areola are both taken in to evenly distribute suckling forces.

312
00:35:50,676 --> 00:36:00,651
Moreover, the force of the hard palate against the lactiferous sinuses aids their efficient emptying, while the nipple is thereby positioned closer to the soft palate.

313
00:36:00,651 --> 00:36:03,989
# Breastfeeding Contraindications.

314
00:36:03,989 --> 00:36:23,139
Lactation is contraindicated in women who take street drugs or do not control their alcohol use; have an infant with galactosemia; have human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection; have active, untreated tuberculosis; take certain medications; or are undergoing breast cancer treatment .

315
00:36:23,139 --> 00:36:33,214
Breastfeeding has been recognized for some time as a mode of HIV transmission and is proscribed in developed countries in which adequate nutrition is otherwise available .

316
00:36:33,214 --> 00:36:37,451
Other viral infections do not contraindicate lactation.

317
00:36:37,451 --> 00:36:45,114
For example, with maternal cytomegalovirus infection, both virus and antibodies are present in breast milk.

318
00:36:45,114 --> 00:36:55,514
And, although hepatitis B virus is excreted in milk, breastfeeding is not contraindicated if hepatitis B immune globulin is given to the newborns of affected mothers.

319
00:36:55,514 --> 00:37:03,014
Maternal hepatitis C infection is not a contraindication because breastfeeding has not been shown to transmit infection.

320
00:37:03,014 --> 00:37:12,464
Women with active herpes simplex virus may suckle their infants if there are no breast lesions and if particular care is directed to hand washing before nursing.

321
00:37:12,464 --> 00:37:19,776
From the CDC (2021a), those with COVID-19 should wear a mask and practice hand-hygiene.

322
00:37:19,776 --> 00:37:22,614
# Drugs Secreted in Milk.

323
00:37:22,614 --> 00:37:29,914
Most drugs given to the mother are secreted in breast milk, although the amount ingested by the infant typically is small.

324
00:37:29,914 --> 00:37:42,464
Factors influencing drug excretion include plasma concentration, degree of protein binding, plasma and milk pH, degree of ionization, lipid solubility, and molecular weight .

325
00:37:42,464 --> 00:37:50,164
The ratio of drug concentration in breast milk to that in maternal plasma is the milk-to-plasma drug-concentration ratio.

326
00:37:50,164 --> 00:38:00,689
Ideally, to minimize infant exposure, medication selection favors drugs with a shorter half-life, poorer oral absorption, and lower lipid solubility.

327
00:38:00,689 --> 00:38:05,326
If multiple daily drug doses are required, each is taken by the

328
00:38:05,476 --> 00:38:08,088
mother after the closest feed.

329
00:38:08,038 --> 00:38:15,038
Single daily-dosed drugs may be taken just before the longest infant sleep interval—usually at bedtime.

330
00:38:15,038 --> 00:38:19,538
Only a few drugs are absolutely contraindicated while breastfeeding .

331
00:38:19,538 --> 00:38:31,651
Cytotoxic drugs may interfere with cellular metabolism and potentially cause immune suppression or neutropenia, affect growth, and at least theoretically, increase the childhood cancer risk.

332
00:38:31,651 --> 00:38:39,076
Examples include cyclophosphamide, cyclosporine, doxorubicin, methotrexate, and mycophenolate .

333
00:38:39,076 --> 00:38:44,038
If a medication presents a concern, its importance should be ascertained.

334
00:38:44,038 --> 00:38:54,551
A provider can also seek a safer alternative or determine if neonatal exposure can be minimized if the medication dose is taken immediately after each breastfeeding session.

335
00:38:54,551 --> 00:39:06,326
Last, recreational drugs such as marijuana and alcohol should be avoided (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2017c; Metz, 2018).

336
00:39:06,326 --> 00:39:22,763
Data on individual drugs are available through the National Institutes of Health website, LactMed, which can be found at www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK501922/.

337
00:39:22,763 --> 00:39:31,238
Radioactive isotopes of copper, gallium, indium, iodine, sodium, and technetium rapidly appear in breast milk.

338
00:39:31,238 --> 00:39:38,701
Consultation with a nuclear medicine specialist is recommended before performing a diagnostic study with these isotopes .

339
00:39:38,701 --> 00:39:44,363
Ideally, a radionuclide with the shortest excretion time in breast milk is selected.

340
00:39:44,363 --> 00:39:50,376
The mother should pump her breasts before the study and store enough milk in a freezer to feed the infant.

341
00:39:50,376 --> 00:39:59,326
After the study, she should pump her breasts to maintain milk production but discard all milk produced during the time that radioactivity is present.

342
00:39:59,326 --> 00:40:04,713
This ranges from 15 hours to 2 weeks, depending on the isotope used.

343
00:40:04,713 --> 00:40:10,276
Importantly, radioactive iodine concentrates and persists in the thyroid.

344
00:40:10,276 --> 00:40:16,538
Its special considerations are discussed in Chapter 66 (p. 1174).

345
00:40:16,538 --> 00:40:23,888
For magnetic resonance (MR) imaging, breastfeeding should not be interrupted after gadolinium administration .

346
00:40:23,888 --> 00:40:26,426
# Breast Engorgement.

347
00:40:26,426 --> 00:40:29,788
This is common in women who do not breastfeed.

348
00:40:29,788 --> 00:40:35,888
It is typified by milk leakage and breast pain, which peak 3 to 5 days after delivery.

349
00:40:35,888 --> 00:40:45,363
Up to half of affected women require analgesia for breast pain relief, and as many as 10 percent report severe pain for up to 14 days.

350
00:40:45,363 --> 00:40:49,963
Evidence is insufficient to firmly support any specific treatment .

351
00:40:49,963 --> 00:40:56,151
That said, breasts can be supported with a well-fitting brassiere, breast binder, or sports bra.

352
00:40:56,151 --> 00:41:01,563
Cool packs and oral analgesics for 12 to 24 hours aid discomfort.

353
00:41:01,563 --> 00:41:07,538
Pharmacological or hormonal agents in general are not recommended to suppress lactation.

354
00:41:07,538 --> 00:41:12,738
Fever caused by breast engorgement was common before the renaissance of breastfeeding.

355
00:41:12,738 --> 00:41:21,538
In one study, 13 percent of puerperas with engorgement had fever ranging from 37.8 to 39°C .

356
00:41:21,538 --> 00:41:25,801
Fever seldom persists for longer than 4 to 16 hours.

357
00:41:25,801 --> 00:41:32,551
The incidence and severity of engorgement and of the fever associated with it are much lower if women breastfeed.

358
00:41:32,551 --> 00:41:37,876
Other causes of fever, especially those due to infection, must be excluded.

359
00:41:37,876 --> 00:41:43,963
Mastitis is infection of the mammary parenchyma and is relatively common in lactating women .

360
00:41:43,963 --> 00:41:46,951
# Other Lactation Issues.

361
00:41:46,951 --> 00:41:53,288
With inverted nipples, lactiferous ducts open directly into a depression at the center of the areola.

362
00:41:53,288 --> 00:41:56,976
With these depressed nipples, nursing is difficult.

363
00:41:56,976 --> 00:42:02,276
If the depression is not deep, milk sometimes can be drawn out by a breast pump.

364
00:42:02,276 --> 00:42:11,238
If instead the nipple is greatly inverted, daily attempts are made during the last few months of pregnancy to draw or “tease” the nipple out with the fingers.

365
00:42:11,238 --> 00:42:19,363
Extra breasts—polymastia, or extra nipples—polythelia, may develop along the former embryonic mammary ridge.

366
00:42:19,363 --> 00:42:25,238
Also termed the milk line, this line extends from the axilla to the groin bilaterally.

367
00:42:25,238 --> 00:42:31,163
In some women, rests of accessory breast tissue can be found in the mons pubis or vulva .

368
00:42:31,163 --> 00:42:38,751
In the general population, the incidence of accessory breast tissue ranges from 0.22 to 6 percent .

369
00:42:38,751 --> 00:42:47,363
These breasts may be so small as to be mistaken for pigmented moles, or if without a nipple, for lymphadenopathy or lipoma.

370
00:42:47,363 --> 00:42:53,163
Polymastia has no obstetrical significance, although occasionally enlargement of

371
00:42:53,332 --> 00:43:00,707
these accessory breasts during pregnancy or engorgement postpartum may result in patient discomfort and anxiety.

372
00:43:00,657 --> 00:43:05,944
Galactocele is a milk duct that becomes obstructed by inspissated secretions.

373
00:43:05,944 --> 00:43:16,207
The amount is ordinarily limited, but an excess may form a fluctuant mass—a galactocele—that can cause pressure symptoms and have the appearance of an abscess.

374
00:43:16,207 --> 00:43:20,494
It may resolve spontaneously or require aspiration.

375
00:43:20,494 --> 00:43:25,294
Among individuals, the volume of milk secreted varies markedly.

376
00:43:25,294 --> 00:43:30,407
This depends not on general maternal health but on breast glandular development.

377
00:43:30,407 --> 00:43:35,144
Rarely, there is complete lack of mammary secretion—agalactia.

378
00:43:35,144 --> 00:43:39,894
Occasionally, mammary secretion is excessive—polygalactia.

379
00:43:39,894 --> 00:43:53,382
Lactation-Associated Osteoporosis This is a rare disorder of unknown etiology that may be associated with severe back pain or vertebral fractures (Li, 2018; Zhang, 2017).

380
00:43:53,382 --> 00:43:58,669
Preliminary studies indicate that bisphosphonates are effective in its management.

381
00:43:58,669 --> 00:44:05,494
However, for almost all women, lactation is not associated with later-onset osteoporosis .

382
00:44:05,494 --> 00:44:12,119
Pregnancy-associated osteoporosis is discussed in Chapter 61 (p. 1100).

383
00:44:12,119 --> 00:44:14,007
Hospital Care.

384
00:44:14,007 --> 00:44:20,582
For 2 hours after delivery, blood pressure and pulse are taken every 15 minutes, at minimum.

385
00:44:20,582 --> 00:44:27,169
Temperature is assessed every 4 hours for the first 8 hours and then at least every 8 hours subsequently .

386
00:44:27,169 --> 00:44:37,269
If regional analgesia or general anesthesia was used for labor or delivery, the mother should be observed in an appropriately equipped and staffed recovery area.

387
00:44:37,269 --> 00:44:44,832
Expected anesthesia recovery and complications are described in Chapter 25 (p. 473).

388
00:44:44,832 --> 00:44:52,407
Because the likelihood of significant hemorrhage is greatest immediately postpartum, vaginal bleeding is closely monitored.

389
00:44:52,407 --> 00:44:57,082
The uterine fundus is palpated to ensure that it is well contracted.

390
00:44:57,082 --> 00:45:04,157
If relaxation is detected, the uterus should be massaged through the abdominal wall until it remains contracted.

391
00:45:04,157 --> 00:45:07,607
Uterotonics also are sometimes required.

392
00:45:07,607 --> 00:45:11,832
Blood may accumulate within the uterus without external bleeding.

393
00:45:11,832 --> 00:45:18,482
This may be detected early by uterine enlargement during fundal palpation in the first postdelivery hours.

394
00:45:18,482 --> 00:45:22,382
Postpartum hemorrhage is discussed in Chapter 41.

395
00:45:22,382 --> 00:45:26,969
Early ambulation within a few hours after delivery is encouraged.

396
00:45:26,969 --> 00:45:32,644
An attendant should be present for at least the first time, in case the woman becomes syncopal.

397
00:45:32,644 --> 00:45:43,607
The many confirmed advantages of early ambulation include fewer bladder complications, less frequent constipation, and reduced rates of puerperal venous thromboembolism.

398
00:45:43,607 --> 00:45:49,719
As discussed, deep-vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism are common in the puerperium.

399
00:45:49,719 --> 00:45:56,944
In our audits of puerperal women at Parkland Hospital, the frequency of venous thromboembolism is extremely low.

400
00:45:56,944 --> 00:45:59,932
We attribute this to early ambulation.

401
00:45:59,932 --> 00:46:08,582
Risk factors and other measures to diminish the frequency of thromboembolism are discussed in Chapter 55 (p. 984).

402
00:46:08,582 --> 00:46:13,032
Diets need not be restricted for women who give birth vaginally.

403
00:46:13,032 --> 00:46:19,269
Generally, two hours after uncomplicated vaginal delivery, a woman is allowed to eat.

404
00:46:19,269 --> 00:46:29,682
With lactation, the level of calories and protein consumed during pregnancy is increased slightly as recommended by the Food and Nutrition Board of the National Research Council .

405
00:46:29,682 --> 00:46:35,882
If the mother does not breastfeed, dietary requirements are the same as for a nonpregnant woman.

406
00:46:35,882 --> 00:46:44,357
We recommend oral iron supplementation for at least 3 months after delivery and hematocrit evaluation at the first postpartum visit.

407
00:46:44,357 --> 00:46:50,232
As noted earlier, profound drops in estrogen levels follow removal of the placenta.

408
00:46:50,232 --> 00:46:56,794
Reminiscent of the menopause, postpartum women may experience hot flushes, especially at night.

409
00:46:56,794 --> 00:47:04,294
Importantly, the patient’s temperature is assessed to differentiate these physiological vasomotor events from infection.

410
00:47:04,294 --> 00:47:09,444
In women with migraines, dramatic hypoestrogenism may trigger headaches.

411
00:47:09,444 --> 00:47:17,082
Importantly, severe headaches should be differentiated from postdural puncture headache or hypertensive complications.

412
00:47:17,082 --> 00:47:20,307
Care varies depending on headache severity.

413
00:47:20,307 --> 00:47:25,857
Mild headaches may respond to analgesics such as ibuprofen or acetaminophen.

414
00:47:25,857 --> 00:47:30,857
For more severe headaches, oral or systemic narcotics can be used.

415
00:47:30,857 --> 00:47:34,257
Alternatively, a triptan, such as

416
00:47:34,420 --> 00:47:42,495
sumatriptan (Imitrex), can relieve headaches by causing intracranial vasoconstriction, and it is breastfeeding compatible.

417
00:47:42,445 --> 00:47:44,907
# Perineal Care.

418
00:47:44,907 --> 00:47:51,170
The woman is instructed to clean the vulva from anterior to posterior—the vulva toward the anus.

419
00:47:51,170 --> 00:48:00,720
A cool pack applied to the perineum may help reduce edema and discomfort during the first 24 hours if there is a perineal laceration or an episiotomy.

420
00:48:00,720 --> 00:48:07,920
Most women also appear to obtain a measure of relief from the periodic application of a local anesthetic spray.

421
00:48:07,920 --> 00:48:14,682
Severe perineal, vaginal, or rectal pain always warrants careful inspection and palpation.

422
00:48:14,682 --> 00:48:29,157
Severe discomfort usually indicates a problem, such as a hematoma within the first day or so and infection after the third or fourth day (Chap. 37, p. 657 and Chap. 42, p. 740).

423
00:48:29,157 --> 00:48:37,682
Beginning approximately 24 hours after delivery, moist heat as provided by warm sitz baths can reduce local discomfort.

424
00:48:37,682 --> 00:48:41,732
Tub bathing after uncomplicated delivery is allowed.

425
00:48:41,732 --> 00:48:47,932
The episiotomy incision normally is firmly healed and nearly asymptomatic by the third week.

426
00:48:47,932 --> 00:48:55,307
Rarely, the cervix, and occasionally a portion of the uterine body, may protrude from the vulva following delivery.

427
00:48:55,307 --> 00:49:01,420
This is accompanied by variable degrees of anterior and posterior vaginal wall prolapse.

428
00:49:01,420 --> 00:49:07,907
Symptoms include a palpable mass at or past the introitus, voiding difficulties, or pressure.

429
00:49:07,907 --> 00:49:14,145
Puerperal procidentia typically improves with time as the weight of the uterus lessens with involution.

430
00:49:14,145 --> 00:49:23,207
As a temporizing measure for pronounced prolapse, the uterus can be replaced and held in position with a space-filling pessary, such as a donut type.

431
00:49:23,207 --> 00:49:26,570
Rectal veins are often congested at term.

432
00:49:26,570 --> 00:49:31,982
Thrombosis of these hemorrhoids is common and may be promoted by second-stage pushing.

433
00:49:31,982 --> 00:49:38,245
Treatment includes topically applied anesthetics, warm soaks, and stoolsoftening agents.

434
00:49:38,245 --> 00:49:45,770
Nonprescription topical preparations containing corticosteroids, astringents, or phenylephrine are often used.

435
00:49:45,770 --> 00:49:51,607
However, no randomized studies support their efficacy compared with symptomatic care.

436
00:49:51,607 --> 00:49:54,020
# Bladder Function.

437
00:49:54,020 --> 00:50:01,757
In most delivery units, intravenous fluids are infused during labor and for an hour or so after vaginal delivery.

438
00:50:01,757 --> 00:50:10,220
Oxytocin, in doses that have an antidiuretic effect, is typically infused postpartum, and rapid bladder filling is common.

439
00:50:10,220 --> 00:50:19,270
Moreover, both bladder sensation and capability to empty spontaneously may be diminished by components of the labor and delivery process.

440
00:50:19,270 --> 00:50:25,295
Thus, urinary retention and bladder overdistention is common in the early puerperium.

441
00:50:25,295 --> 00:50:31,620
The incidence in more than 5500 women studied with a bladder scanner was 5 percent .

442
00:50:31,620 --> 00:50:33,945
It is much less clinically.

443
00:50:33,945 --> 00:50:53,907
Risk factors that increased the likelihood of retention include primiparity, epidural analgesia, cesarean delivery, perineal lacerations, operative vaginal delivery, catheterization during labor, and prolonged second-stage labor (Stephansson, 2016; Wang, 2017).

444
00:50:53,907 --> 00:51:03,445
Prevention of bladder overdistention demands observation after delivery to ensure that the bladder does not overfill and that it empties adequately with each voiding.

445
00:51:03,445 --> 00:51:12,407
The enlarged bladder can be palpated suprapubically, or it is evident abdominally indirectly as it elevates the fundus above the umbilicus.

446
00:51:12,407 --> 00:51:20,082
Most currently use an automated sonography system to detect high bladder volumes and thus postpartum urinary retention .

447
00:51:20,082 --> 00:51:25,907
If a woman has not voided within 4 hours after delivery, it is likely that she cannot.

448
00:51:25,907 --> 00:51:31,020
If she has trouble voiding initially, she also is likely to have further trouble.

449
00:51:31,020 --> 00:51:38,470
First, an examination for perineal and genital-tract hematomas is completed, as these may be contributory.

450
00:51:38,470 --> 00:51:46,270
With an overdistended bladder, an indwelling catheter should be placed and left until the factors causing retention have abated.

451
00:51:46,270 --> 00:51:53,545
Even without a demonstrable cause, it usually is best to leave the catheter in place for at least 24 hours.

452
00:51:53,545 --> 00:51:59,020
This prevents recurrence and allows recovery of normal bladder tone and sensation.

453
00:51:59,020 --> 00:52:05,632
When the catheter is removed, a voiding trial is completed to demonstrate an ability to void appropriately.

454
00:52:05,632 --> 00:52:11,470
If a woman cannot void after 4 hours, urine volumes are measured sonographically.

455
00:52:11,470 --> 00:52:13,670
If more than 200

456
00:52:13,828 --> 00:52:21,640
mL, the bladder is not functioning appropriately, and the catheter is replaced and remains for another 24 hours.

457
00:52:21,590 --> 00:52:32,940
Although rare, if retention persists after a second voiding trial, an indwelling catheter and leg bag can be elected, and the patient returns in 1 week for an outpatient voiding trial.

458
00:52:32,940 --> 00:52:40,090
In a study of 27 women with a protracted course, all resumed normal voiding by 3 weeks postpartum .

459
00:52:40,090 --> 00:52:51,890
During a voiding trial, if less than 200 mL of urine is obtained, the catheter can be removed and the bladder subsequently monitored clinically and sonographically as described earlier.

460
00:52:51,890 --> 00:53:06,928
Harris and coworkers (1977) reported that 40 percent of such women develop bacteriuria, and thus a single dose or short course of antimicrobial therapy against uropathogens is reasonable after the catheter is removed.

461
00:53:06,928 --> 00:53:10,528
# Pain, Mood, and Cognition.

462
00:53:10,528 --> 00:53:17,878
Discomfort and its causes following cesarean delivery are considered in Chapter 30 (p. 565).

463
00:53:17,878 --> 00:53:30,690
During the first few days after vaginal delivery, the mother may be uncomfortable because of afterpains, episiotomy and lacerations, breast engorgement, and at times, postdural puncture headache.

464
00:53:30,690 --> 00:53:41,865
Mild analgesics containing codeine, ibuprofen, or acetaminophen, preferably in combinations, are given as frequently as every 4 hours during the first few days.

465
00:53:41,865 --> 00:53:45,828
It is important to screen the postpartum woman for depression .

466
00:53:45,828 --> 00:53:51,303
Commonly, mothers exhibit some degree of depressed mood a few days after delivery.

467
00:53:51,303 --> 00:53:56,565
Termed postpartum blues, this likely is the consequence of several factors.

468
00:53:56,565 --> 00:54:13,278
These include the emotional letdown that follows the excitement and fears experienced during pregnancy and delivery, discomforts of the early puerperium, fatigue from sleep deprivation, anxiety over the ability to provide appropriate newborn care, and body image concerns.

469
00:54:13,278 --> 00:54:19,565
In most women, effective treatment includes anticipation, recognition, and reassurance.

470
00:54:19,565 --> 00:54:27,165
This disorder is usually mild and self-limited to 2 to 3 days, although it sometimes lasts for up to 10 days.

471
00:54:27,165 --> 00:54:33,415
Should these moods persist or worsen, an evaluation for symptoms of major depression is done .

472
00:54:33,415 --> 00:54:38,803
Last, postpartum hormonal changes in some women may affect brain function.

473
00:54:38,803 --> 00:54:46,403
Bannbers and colleagues (2013) observed a functional decline in executive function in postpartum women.

474
00:54:46,403 --> 00:54:49,878
# Neuromusculoskeletal Problems.

475
00:54:49,878 --> 00:55:03,990
Obstetrical Neuropathies Pressure on branches of the lumbosacral nerve plexus during labor may manifest as complaints of intense neuralgia or cramplike pains extending down one or both legs as soon as the head descends into the pelvis.

476
00:55:03,990 --> 00:55:12,453
If the nerve is injured, pain may continue after delivery, and variable degrees of sensory loss or muscle paralysis can result.

477
00:55:12,453 --> 00:55:23,090
In some cases, there is footdrop, which can be secondary to injury at the level of the lumbosacral plexus, sciatic nerve, or common fibular (peroneal) nerve .

478
00:55:23,090 --> 00:55:30,278
Components of the lumbosacral plexus cross the pelvic brim and can be compressed by the fetal head or by forceps.

479
00:55:30,278 --> 00:55:38,715
The common fibular nerves may be externally compressed when the legs are positioned in stirrups, especially during prolonged second-stage labor.

480
00:55:38,715 --> 00:55:42,503
Obstetrical neuropathy is relatively infrequent.

481
00:55:42,503 --> 00:55:49,815
Evaluation of more than 6000 puerperas found that approximately 1 percent had a confirmed nerve injury .

482
00:55:49,815 --> 00:55:58,465
Lateral femoral cutaneous neuropathies were the most common (24 percent), followed by femoral neuropathies (14 percent).

483
00:55:58,465 --> 00:56:02,003
A motor deficit accompanied a third of injuries.

484
00:56:02,003 --> 00:56:09,878
Nulliparity, prolonged secondstage labor, and pushing for a long duration in the semi-Fowler position were risk factors.

485
00:56:09,878 --> 00:56:15,928
The median duration of symptoms was 2 months, and the range was 2 weeks to 18 months.

486
00:56:15,928 --> 00:56:26,265
Injury of the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves may occur with cesarean delivery (Rahn, 2010; Yazici Yilmaz, 2018).

487
00:56:26,265 --> 00:56:34,328
We have rarely encountered lumbar arachnoiditis following epidural analgesia causing severe bilateral neuropathic pain.

488
00:56:34,328 --> 00:56:44,565
Musculoskeletal Injuries Pain in the pelvic girdle, hips, or lower extremities may follow stretching or tearing injuries sustained at normal or difficult delivery.

489
00:56:44,565 --> 00:56:49,540
MR imaging is often informative when clinical examination is normal .

490
00:56:49,540 --> 00:56:51,903
Most injuries resolve

491
00:56:52,060 --> 00:56:56,122
with antiinflammatory agents and physical therapy.

492
00:56:56,072 --> 00:57:05,472
Rarely, there may be septic pyomyositis such as with iliopsoas muscle abscess (Nelson, 2010; Young, 2010).

493
00:57:05,472 --> 00:57:16,135
Separation of the symphysis pubis or one of the sacroiliac synchondroses during labor leads to pain and marked interference with locomotion (Fig. 36- 7).

494
00:57:16,135 --> 00:57:26,935
Estimates of the frequency of this event vary widely from 1 in 600 to 1 in 30,000 deliveries (Reis, 1932; Taylor, 1986).

495
00:57:26,935 --> 00:57:31,522
In our experiences, symptomatic separations are uncommon.

496
00:57:31,522 --> 00:57:40,222
Their onset of pain is often acute during delivery, but symptoms may manifest either antepartum or up to 48 hours postpartum .

497
00:57:40,222 --> 00:57:44,022
Radiography is typically used for evaluation.

498
00:57:44,022 --> 00:57:55,497
The normal distance of the symphyseal joint is 0.4 to 0.5 cm, and symphyseal separation >1 cm is diagnostic for diastasis.

499
00:57:55,497 --> 00:58:03,135
Treatment is generally conservative, with rest in a lateral decubitus position and an appropriately fitted pelvic binder .

500
00:58:03,135 --> 00:58:09,622
Surgery is occasionally necessary in some symphyseal separations >4 cm .

501
00:58:09,622 --> 00:58:19,435
The recurrence risk is high in subsequent pregnancies, and Culligan and associates (2002) recommend consideration for cesarean delivery.

502
00:58:19,435 --> 00:58:22,097
Figure 36-7.

503
00:58:22,097 --> 00:58:30,047
Pubic symphyseal separation found on the first postpartum day following vaginal delivery of a 2840-g newborn.

504
00:58:30,047 --> 00:58:34,472
The patient had pain over the pubic bone and pain with ambulation.

505
00:58:34,472 --> 00:58:39,947
A shuffling gait was noted, and she had difficulty with leg elevation when supine.

506
00:58:39,947 --> 00:58:44,222
The patient was treated with physical therapy and analgesics.

507
00:58:44,222 --> 00:58:48,597
A pelvic binder was applied, and a rolling walker was provided.

508
00:58:48,597 --> 00:58:53,297
She improved quickly and was discharged home on postpartum day 5.

509
00:58:53,297 --> 00:59:04,835
In rare cases, fractures of the sacrum or pubic ramus are caused by even uncomplicated deliveries (Alonso-Burgos, 2007; Speziali, 2015).

510
00:59:04,835 --> 00:59:14,760
Discussed in Chapter 61 (p. 1100), the latter is more likely with osteoporosis associated with heparin or corticosteroid therapy.

511
00:59:14,760 --> 00:59:21,860
In rare but serious cases, bacterial osteomyelitis—osteitis pubis—can be devastating.

512
00:59:21,860 --> 00:59:28,310
Lawford and coworkers (2010) reported such a case that caused massive vulvar edema.

513
00:59:28,310 --> 00:59:30,822
# Immunizations.

514
00:59:30,822 --> 00:59:40,772
The D-negative woman who is not isoimmunized and whose newborn is Dpositive is given 300 μg of anti-D immune globulin shortly after delivery .

515
00:59:40,772 --> 00:59:48,197
Women who are not already immune to rubella or varicella are excellent candidates for vaccination before discharge .

516
00:59:48,197 --> 00:59:59,210
Those who have not received a tetanus/diphtheria (Tdap/Td) or influenza vaccine should be given these (Table 10-7, p. 189).

517
00:59:59,210 --> 01:00:06,660
The CDC (2021b) recommends the COVID-19 vaccine, and includes breastfeeding women.

518
01:00:06,660 --> 01:00:19,697
Morgan and colleagues (2015) reported that implementation of a bestpractices alert in the electronic medical record was associated with a Tdap immunization rate of 97 percent at Parkland Hospital.

519
01:00:19,697 --> 01:00:30,322
When permissible by law, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019) recommends standing orders for indicated immunizations.

520
01:00:30,322 --> 01:00:32,747
# Contraception.

521
01:00:32,747 --> 01:00:38,997
During the hospital stay, a concerted effort is made to provide family planning education.

522
01:00:38,997 --> 01:00:46,835
Various forms of contraception are discussed throughout Chapter 38 and sterilization procedures in Chapter 39.

523
01:00:46,835 --> 01:00:53,935
The immediate puerperium is an ideal time for consideration of long-acting reversible contraception—LARC .

524
01:00:53,935 --> 01:00:59,110
Women not breastfeeding have return of menses usually within 6 to 8 weeks.

525
01:00:59,110 --> 01:01:06,735
At times, however, it is difficult clinically to assign a specific date to the first menstrual period after delivery.

526
01:01:06,735 --> 01:01:12,835
A minority of women bleed small to moderate amounts intermittently starting soon after delivery.

527
01:01:12,835 --> 01:01:18,347
Ovulation occurs at a mean of 7 weeks but ranges from 5 to 11 weeks .

528
01:01:18,347 --> 01:01:22,910
That said, ovulation before 28 days has been described .

529
01:01:22,910 --> 01:01:27,310
Thus, conception is possible during the early puerperium.

530
01:01:27,310 --> 01:01:34,735
Women who become sexually active during the puerperium and who do not desire to conceive should initiate contraception.

531
01:01:34,735 --> 01:01:47,485
Kelly and associates (2005) reported that by the third month postpartum, 58 percent of adolescents had resumed sexual intercourse, but only 80 percent of these were using contraception.

532
01:01:47,485 --> 01:01:51,785
Because of this, many recommend LARC during the puerperium.

533
01:01:51,785 --> 01:01:56,172
Women who breastfeed ovulate much less frequently compared with

534
01:01:56,332 --> 01:01:59,769
those who do not, but variation is great.

535
01:01:59,719 --> 01:02:06,219
Timing of ovulation depends on individual biological variation and the intensity of breastfeeding.

536
01:02:06,219 --> 01:02:12,944
Lactating women may first menstruate as early as the second or as late as the 18th month after delivery.

537
01:02:12,944 --> 01:02:20,132
Campbell and Gray (1993) analyzed daily urine specimens in 92 lactating women.

538
01:02:20,132 --> 01:02:26,582
Breastfeeding in general delays resumption of ovulation, although it does not invariably forestall it.

539
01:02:26,582 --> 01:02:35,194
Other findings in their study included the following: 1. Resumption of ovulation was frequently marked by return of normal menstrual bleeding.

540
01:02:35,194 --> 01:02:41,994
2. Breastfeeding episodes lasting 15 minutes seven times daily delayed ovulation resumption.

541
01:02:41,994 --> 01:02:45,394
3. Ovulation can occur without bleeding.

542
01:02:45,394 --> 01:02:48,419
4. Bleeding can be anovulatory.

543
01:02:48,419 --> 01:02:54,557
5. The risk of pregnancy in breastfeeding women was approximately 4 percent per year.

544
01:02:54,557 --> 01:03:07,657
For the breastfeeding woman, progestin-only contraceptives, such as progestin pills, depot medroxyprogesterone, or progestin implants or IUDs, do not affect the quality or quantity of milk.

545
01:03:07,657 --> 01:03:14,907
Not available in the United States, success with the progesterone-releasing vaginal ring also has been described .

546
01:03:14,907 --> 01:03:18,957
These may be initiated any time during the puerperium.

547
01:03:18,957 --> 01:03:28,657
Estrogen-progestin contraceptives likely reduce the quantity of breast milk, but under the proper circumstances, they too can be used by lactating women.

548
01:03:28,657 --> 01:03:34,182
These hormonal methods are discussed in Chapter 38 (p. 671).

549
01:03:34,182 --> 01:03:36,894
# Hospital Discharge.

550
01:03:36,894 --> 01:03:44,107
Following uncomplicated vaginal delivery, hospitalization is seldom warranted for more than 48 hours.

551
01:03:44,107 --> 01:03:49,732
Hospital stay length following labor and delivery is now regulated by federal law .

552
01:03:49,732 --> 01:04:09,957
Currently, the norms are hospital stays up to 48 hours following uncomplicated vaginal delivery and up to 96 hours following uncomplicated cesarean delivery (American Academy of Pediatrics and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2017; Blumenfield, 2015).

553
01:04:09,957 --> 01:04:15,869
Earlier hospital discharge is acceptable for appropriately selected women if they desire it.

554
01:04:15,869 --> 01:04:27,057
A woman should receive instructions concerning anticipated normal physiological puerperal changes, including lochia patterns, weight loss from diuresis, and milk let-down.

555
01:04:27,057 --> 01:04:34,969
She also should receive instructions concerning fever, excessive vaginal bleeding, or leg pain, swelling, or tenderness.

556
01:04:34,969 --> 01:04:40,494
Persistent headaches, shortness of breath, or chest pain warrant immediate concern.

557
01:04:40,494 --> 01:04:42,132
Home Care.

558
01:04:42,132 --> 01:04:44,269
# Coitus.

559
01:04:44,269 --> 01:04:50,982
No evidence-based data guide resumption of coitus after delivery, and practices are individualized .

560
01:04:50,982 --> 01:04:55,957
After 2 weeks, coitus may be resumed based on desire and comfort.

561
01:04:55,957 --> 01:05:05,719
Wallwiener and colleagues (2017) reported that 60 percent of women resumed sexual activity by 1 week and 80 percent by 4 months.

562
01:05:05,719 --> 01:05:10,132
They also reported that a third of these women had sexual dysfunction.

563
01:05:10,132 --> 01:05:19,044
Intercourse too soon may be unpleasant, if not painful, and this may be related to episiotomy incisions or perineal lacerations.

564
01:05:19,044 --> 01:05:27,482
In a study of women without an episiotomy, only 0.4 percent of those with a first- or seconddegree tear had dyspareunia .

565
01:05:27,482 --> 01:05:39,057
Conversely, in primiparas with an episiotomy, 67 percent had sexual dysfunction at 3 months, 31 percent at 6 months, and 15 percent at 12 months .

566
01:05:39,057 --> 01:05:45,232
Only 40 percent of women with an anal sphincter injury had resumed intercourse by 12 weeks .

567
01:05:45,232 --> 01:05:50,169
Last, dyspareunia was also common following cesarean delivery .

568
01:05:50,169 --> 01:05:57,557
Postpartum, the vulvovaginal epithelium is thin, and very little lubrication follows sexual stimulation.

569
01:05:57,557 --> 01:06:04,432
This stems from the hypoestrogenic state following delivery, which lasts until ovulation resumes.

570
01:06:04,432 --> 01:06:11,319
It may be particularly problematic in breastfeeding women who are hypoestrogenic for many months postpartum .

571
01:06:11,319 --> 01:06:18,594
For treatment, small amounts of topical estrogen cream can be applied daily for several weeks to vulvar tissues.

572
01:06:18,594 --> 01:06:22,857
Additionally, vaginal lubricants may be used with coitus.

573
01:06:22,857 --> 01:06:27,682
This same thinning of the vulvovaginal epithelium can lead to dysuria.

574
01:06:27,682 --> 01:06:32,494
Topical estrogen can again be offered once cystitis is excluded.

575
01:06:32,494 --> 01:06:34,969
# Follow-Up Care.

576
01:06:34,969 --> 01:06:44,469
By discharge, women who had an uncomplicated vaginal delivery can resume most activities, including bathing, driving, and household functions.

577
01:06:44,469 --> 01:06:46,732
Despite this, Tulman

578
01:06:46,900 --> 01:06:54,650
and Fawcett (1988) reported that only half of mothers regained their usual level of energy by 6 weeks.

579
01:06:54,600 --> 01:07:02,575
Women who delivered vaginally were twice as likely to have normal energy levels at this time compared with those with a cesarean delivery.

580
01:07:02,575 --> 01:07:08,987
Ideally, the care and nurturing of the infant should be provided by the mother with ample help from the father.

581
01:07:08,987 --> 01:07:19,350
Jimenez and Newton (1979) tabulated cross-cultural information on 202 societies from various international geographical regions.

582
01:07:19,350 --> 01:07:28,662
Following childbirth, most societies did not restrict work activity, and approximately half expected a return to full duties within 2 weeks.

583
01:07:28,662 --> 01:07:37,625
Wallace and coworkers (2013) reported that 80 percent of women who worked during pregnancy resume work by 1 year after delivery.

584
01:07:37,625 --> 01:07:51,237
Discussed on page 634, during this fourth trimester, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a) recommends a comprehensive visit within 12 weeks after delivery.

585
01:07:51,237 --> 01:08:00,275
This has proved quite satisfactory to identify abnormalities beyond the immediate puerperium and to initiate contraceptive practices.

